## Topic Understanding

Finding a therapist is an important step toward caring for your mental health, but understanding your insurance coverage can sometimes make the process feel complicated. If you have a Medicare Advantage plan and are looking for counseling in Houston, you may be wondering which providers are covered, what therapy might cost, and how to confirm your benefits before scheduling an appointment.

**Riaz Counseling accepts certain Medicare Advantage plans, but coverage depends on your specific plan, provider network, and the services you need.** Verifying your benefits before beginning therapy can help you understand your options and reduce the risk of unexpected costs.

This guide explains how Medicare Advantage coverage for mental health care works, what to ask when searching for Medicare Advantage providers, and how to explore counseling options through Riaz Counseling.

## Key Highlights

- Medicare Advantage plans generally cover Medicare-covered mental health services, but network rules, copayments, and other requirements depend on the individual plan.
- Riaz Counseling has published information about accepting certain Medicare Advantage plans; this does not mean every plan is accepted.
- HealthSpring's 2026 Texas and Oklahoma PPO provider directory lists Manahil Riaz as a Licensed Professional Counselor under behavioral health providers. Members should still confirm their individual coverage.
- Before scheduling, ask about network participation, covered therapy services, referrals or authorizations, and your expected out-of-pocket costs.
- Riaz Counseling serves people seeking mental health support in the Houston area and offers online therapy for Texas residents, subject to service and coverage requirements.

## What Is Medicare Advantage?

Medicare Advantage, also called Medicare Part C, is an alternative way to receive Medicare benefits through a private health insurance plan approved by Medicare.

These plans must cover Medicare-covered services under applicable Medicare rules, although they may use different provider networks, cost-sharing arrangements, and procedures for accessing care.

For someone seeking therapy, the important question is not only whether a plan includes mental health benefits. It is also whether the specific provider and service meet that plan's requirements.

Medicare explains that people enrolled in Medicare Advantage should check with their plan to determine whether a provider is in-network. Some plans allow out-of-network care under certain conditions, but members may pay a larger share of the cost.

### Does Medicare Advantage cover mental health counseling?

Medicare covers several outpatient mental health services, including individual and group psychotherapy and certain family counseling services. Covered services must meet applicable program requirements.

Medicare Advantage plans generally provide coverage for Medicare-covered services, but the exact process and costs depend on the plan.

For example, your plan may have specific rules concerning:

- Which mental health professionals are in-network
- Copayments or coinsurance
- Deductibles
- Referrals or prior authorization, when applicable
- Outpatient therapy benefits
- Telehealth services
- Out-of-network providers

Do not assume that a service is covered at the same rate across every Medicare Advantage plan. Confirm the details with your insurer and the counseling practice.

## Finding Medicare Advantage Providers for Mental Health Care

Finding a suitable therapist involves more than locating a name in an insurance directory. You also need to confirm that the provider's services fit your needs and that your plan will cover the care you intend to receive.

A practical approach is to follow these steps.

### 1. Identify Your Exact Medicare Advantage Plan

Start with your insurance card. Find the full plan name and the insurer's member services contact information.

If you have HealthSpring Medicare Advantage, for example, make sure you identify the specific plan rather than relying on the HealthSpring name alone.

Different plans may have different networks and benefits, even when they are offered by the same insurance company.

### 2. Search Your Plan's Provider Directory

Use your insurer's official provider directory to search for behavioral health professionals.

Depending on your plan, you may be able to search for providers by:

- Name
- Location
- Specialty
- Provider type
- Behavioral health services

If you are searching for Medicare Advantage mental health providers in Houston, narrow the results to the area where you prefer to receive care.

Remember that a directory listing is a useful starting point, not a guarantee that every service will be covered.

### 3. Confirm the Provider's Current Network Status

Before scheduling, contact the provider or the insurer and ask whether the clinician participates in your exact plan.

This matters because provider participation can change, and a provider may participate in one network without participating in another.

For example, Riaz Counseling's published HealthSpring information identifies Manahil Riaz in the 2026 HealthSpring Texas and Oklahoma PPO provider directory. If you have a different HealthSpring plan or another Medicare Advantage insurer, you should confirm your own network status rather than assuming that the listing applies to you.

### 4. Confirm That the Service You Need Is Covered

Not all counseling services necessarily receive the same coverage treatment.

When contacting a practice, explain the type of care you are seeking. You might be looking for individual therapy for anxiety, support with depression, help navigating grief, or assistance managing a difficult life transition.

Ask whether your specific service is covered under your plan and whether any additional requirements apply.

If you are considering couples or family counseling, ask specifically how the plan handles that service. Coverage for one type of psychotherapy does not automatically establish coverage for every relationship or family service.

### 5. Understand Your Expected Costs

Even when a provider is in-network, you may have out-of-pocket expenses.

Depending on your plan and benefits, these may include:

- A copayment for each appointment
- Coinsurance
- A deductible
- Costs for services that are not covered
- Different cost-sharing for out-of-network care, if permitted

Ask the insurer and practice to clarify what they can verify about your benefits before your first session.

You should also ask what happens if your plan changes during the year. A provider who participates in your current network may not participate in a future plan.

